**Behavior summary**

| Gesture | Pinned rows | Project selector (sidebar) |
|---|---|---|
| Plain click | switch active project (unchanged) | switch active project (unchanged) |
| Ctrl/Cmd + click | opens `${origin}/${project_name}` in a new tab, current tab untouched | same |
| Middle click (`auxclick`) | same | same |
| Ctrl/Cmd + click on the *active* project | opens the tab (browser-standard) | opens the tab (browser-standard) |

**Notes / trade-offs**

- Other `ProjectDetailt` usages (`TopBar`, `NavigationBar`, `KnowledgeView`, `AgentPlanView`) ignore the new second `select` argument, so they keep switching in place — no accidental navigation where the selector only picks a project for a local query.
- `openProjectInNewTab` uses `'_blank'` + `noopener` so every gesture really creates a tab (no named-window reuse as in `AppBar.vue`) and the opener is never leaked.
- One caveat: ctrl+click does not "restore" a desktop shell layout — it opens a fresh document at `/<project_name>`, exactly like the existing `window.location.href` switch.

